This is not a false positive. Mypy correctly flags an LSP violation. The superclass guarantees that `method` accepts *any* `Other | Super` argument. However, your second `Sub.method` overload only accepts `Super`, rejecting `Other`. 

If a caller uses a `Super` type hint but passes an `Other` instance, the superclass contract is satisfied, but the subclass overloads have no matching signature, breaking substitutability.

To fix this, the overloads must collectively cover the exact domain of the superclass parameter (`Other | Super`). Since `Other` is a subset of `Other | Sub`, you must explicitly include an overload for `Other`:

```python
class Sub(Super):
    @overload
    def method(self, arg: Other) -> Sub: ...
    @overload
    def method(self, arg: Sub) -> Sub: ...
    @overload
    def method(self, arg: Super) -> Super: ...
```

This ensures `Sub.method` accepts all inputs that `Super.method` does, satisfying LSP.
